---
import BaseLayout from '../components/layout/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config/site';

/**
 * This policy describes what the site actually does, established by reading the
 * codebase rather than by adapting a template. If the implementation changes,
 * change this page in the same commit.
 *
 * Two things described here are prepared but not yet connected, and are
 * described as such rather than as live: the research questionnaire's Formspree
 * endpoint (`src/pages/compare-notes/questionnaire.astro`) and the MailerLite
 * form (`src/config/mailerlite.ts`).
 */
const lastUpdated = '8 August 2026';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy policy"
  description="How Editorial Intelligence collects, uses and protects personal information — email updates, research participation, analytics and your data protection rights."
  suppressFlagshipJourney={true}
>
  <header class="p-hero">
    <div class="container-site">
      <p class="t-label mb-6">Privacy</p>
      <h1 class="t-display mb-6">Privacy policy</h1>
      <p class="p-lede">
        This explains what personal information Editorial Intelligence collects, why, who else
        handles it and what you can ask me to do about it. It is written to be read rather than
        skimmed past.
      </p>
      <p class="p-updated">Last updated {lastUpdated}</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="container-site">
    <article class="p-body">

      <section class="p-section">
        <h2>Who operates Editorial Intelligence</h2>
        <p>
          Editorial Intelligence is operated by {SITE.author}, an independent practitioner based in
          the United Kingdom. There is no company, team or parent organisation behind it — one
          person is responsible for this website, the research programme and everything described
          on this page.
        </p>
        <p>
          For data protection purposes, that makes me the data controller for the personal
          information described here. You can reach me at
          <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="p-section">
        <h2>What this site collects</h2>
        <p>
          Most of this website collects nothing at all. It is a static site: you can read every
          framework, narrative, method and field note without giving me any information and without
          anything being stored on your device.
        </p>
        <p>Personal information reaches me in only three ways, and each one is something you choose to do:</p>
        <ul class="p-list">
          <li>You subscribe to email updates.</li>
          <li>You complete the practitioner research questionnaire.</li>
          <li>You email me directly, or ask for a Compare Notes conversation.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Alongside those, the site uses privacy-focused analytics that count visits without
          identifying visitors. That is described further down.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="p-section">
        <h2>Email updates</h2>
        <p>
          If you subscribe to Editorial Intelligence updates, I collect your email address. Nothing
          else is required — there is no name field, no organisation field and no way to make the
          form ask for more.
        </p>
        <p>
          I use it to send occasional updates about new research, findings as they emerge and
          opportunities to take part. Occasional means what it says: updates go out when the work
          produces something worth sending, not on a schedule.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The lawful basis is your consent.</strong> You give it by ticking the consent box
          on the form, which is never pre-ticked. I keep a record of that consent so I can show it
          was given.
        </p>
        <p>
          The subscription list is held by MailerLite, described under third-party services below.
          You can unsubscribe from any email, at any time, using the link in its footer. That
          removes you immediately and needs no explanation or reply from you.
        </p>
        <p>
          I do not sell, rent or share the list with anyone, and I do not use it for advertising or
          pass it to advertising networks.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="p-section">
        <h2>Research participation</h2>
        <p>
          The Editorial Intelligence Research Project investigates how organisations turn evidence
          into ideas that influence decisions. You can take part by completing the practitioner
          questionnaire or by having a Compare Notes conversation.
        </p>
        <p>
          The questionnaire collects your answers about how your organisation works. Your name,
          email address and organisation are optional, and only needed if you want me to be able to
          follow up. The questionnaire page carries its own detailed privacy notice covering how
          that specific research data is handled, and the permissions you choose there govern what
          happens to your response.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The lawful basis is your consent</strong>, given through the explicit permission
          choices in the questionnaire. You choose whether your response may be quoted anonymously,
          whether I may contact you and whether you are open to a follow-up conversation. Nothing is
          ever attributed to you or your organisation without asking you separately, with the exact
          proposed wording.
        </p>
        <p>
          I should also be straightforward about something the questionnaire states plainly:
          Editorial Intelligence is how I make a living, so this research develops a commercial
          practice as well as a body of knowledge. Responses are read with an eye to where the
          method might be useful to an organisation. No approach about working together follows from
          that unless you raise it first.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="p-section p-section--emphasis">
        <h2>Research consent and email consent are separate</h2>
        <p>
          These are two different decisions and I keep them that way.
        </p>
        <p>
          Taking part in research does not subscribe you to email updates. Completing the
          questionnaire, asking to hear when findings are published, or having a Compare Notes
          conversation will never add you to the mailing list. Research contact details are held
          separately from the subscriber list and the two are not combined.
        </p>
        <p>
          The reverse holds too. Subscribing to updates does not enrol you in the research or give
          me permission to use anything you have said as research data.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you want both, you have to choose both — deliberately, in two places.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="p-section">
        <h2>Why I process this information</h2>
        <ul class="p-list">
          <li><strong>To send email updates</strong> you have asked for.</li>
          <li><strong>To carry out and publish the research</strong>, on the terms you chose when you took part.</li>
          <li><strong>To reply to you</strong> when you get in touch, and to arrange conversations you have agreed to.</li>
          <li><strong>To understand how the site is used</strong> in aggregate, so I can tell which work is worth continuing.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          I do not build profiles of individuals, make automated decisions about anyone, or use
          personal information for advertising or targeting.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="p-section">
        <h2>Cookies and analytics</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>This site does not set any cookies.</strong> There is no cookie banner because
          there is nothing to consent to.
        </p>
        <p>
          The site uses Vercel Web Analytics, which counts page views and referrers without cookies
          and without collecting information that identifies you personally. It gives me visit
          counts, not visitors.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two small pieces of information are stored in your browser's local storage, on your own
          device. Neither is sent anywhere, and neither is a cookie:
        </p>
        <ul class="p-list">
          <li>
            <strong>Your assessment answers.</strong> The capability assessment runs entirely in
            your browser. Your answers are saved locally so you can leave and come back, and are
            never transmitted to me or anyone else. Clearing your browser data deletes them. I
            never see your result unless you choose to tell me.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>An analytics opt-out flag</strong> that I use to keep my own visits out of the
            statistics.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="p-section">
        <h2>Third-party services</h2>
        <p>
          Running a website means relying on other companies. These are the ones that can encounter
          your information, and what each one does:
        </p>

        <div class="p-processors">
          <div class="p-processor">
            <p class="p-processor-name">Vercel</p>
            <p>
              Hosts the website and provides the analytics described above. Because Vercel serves
              every page, its servers process your IP address as part of delivering the site to you,
              as any web host must.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-processor">
            <p class="p-processor-name">MailerLite</p>
            <p>
              Stores the email subscriber list, sends the updates, records consent and handles
              unsubscribes. If you subscribe, your email address is held there.
            </p>
            <p>
              MailerLite offers a tracking script that most sites install on every page.
              <strong>It is deliberately not installed here.</strong> It would set cookies, and the
              no-cookie position above is worth more than the analytics it would provide. The signup
              form posts your address directly to MailerLite when you submit it, and nothing about
              MailerLite loads on any page you merely read.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-processor">
            <p class="p-processor-name">Formspree</p>
            <p>
              Receives research questionnaire submissions and passes them to me. The website is
              static and stores nothing itself, so a form service is what makes the questionnaire
              possible.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-processor">
            <p class="p-processor-name">Google Fonts</p>
            <p>
              Serves the typefaces the site uses. Fonts are requested from Google's servers when a
              page loads, which means Google receives your IP address at that moment. This happens
              on every page, whether or not you interact with anything.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          Some of these providers operate outside the UK, including in the United States. Where
          information is transferred internationally, it is done under the safeguards those
          providers put in place for UK and European data protection law.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each of these companies has its own privacy policy governing what it does with the
          information it handles.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="p-section">
        <h2>How long information is kept</h2>
        <ul class="p-list">
          <li>
            <strong>Email subscriptions</strong> are kept until you unsubscribe or ask me to delete
            your address. There is no reason for me to hold an address once you no longer want the
            updates.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Research responses</strong> are kept for the duration of the research programme
            and deleted when they are no longer needed for it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Emails you send me</strong> are kept as long as the conversation is useful, and
            deleted when it is not.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          One limitation is worth being honest about. Once research responses have been aggregated
          or anonymised into published findings, your individual response can still be deleted, but
          it may no longer be possible to remove its influence from findings already published.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="p-section">
        <h2>Unsubscribing</h2>
        <p>
          Every email includes an unsubscribe link in its footer. One click, no reason needed, no
          reply from me required. It takes effect immediately.
        </p>
        <p>
          If a link ever fails, email <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a> and I will
          remove you by hand.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="p-section">
        <h2>Your data protection rights</h2>
        <p>
          Under UK data protection law you have the right to:
        </p>
        <ul class="p-list">
          <li><strong>Access</strong> the personal information I hold about you, and get a copy.</li>
          <li><strong>Correct</strong> anything inaccurate or incomplete.</li>
          <li><strong>Delete</strong> it — sometimes called the right to be forgotten.</li>
          <li><strong>Restrict</strong> or <strong>object to</strong> how I use it.</li>
          <li><strong>Withdraw consent</strong> at any time, without affecting anything done before you withdrew it.</li>
          <li><strong>Portability</strong> — receive your information in a transferable format.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          To exercise any of these, email <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>. Say what
          you want and I will do it. I will respond within one month, and there is no charge.
        </p>
        <p>
          Given the scale of this operation, most requests are quick. If you ask me to delete your
          information, I will delete it rather than look for reasons to keep it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="p-section">
        <h2>Complaints</h2>
        <p>
          If you think I have handled your information badly, please tell me first — most problems
          are faster to fix directly.
        </p>
        <p>
          You also have the right to complain to the Information Commissioner's Office, the UK's
          data protection regulator, at <a href="https://ico.org.uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ico.org.uk</a>.
          You do not have to raise it with me before going to them.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="p-section">
        <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
        <p>
          If what the site does changes, this page changes with it, and the date at the top updates.
          The website's source code is public, so what this page claims can be checked against what
          the site actually does.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="p-section">
        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>
          For anything about privacy, your information or this policy, email
          <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>. It reaches me directly.
        </p>
      </section>

    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .p-hero {
    padding-block: clamp(4rem, 9vw, 7rem) clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4rem);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
  }

  .p-hero h1 {
    max-width: 18ch;
  }

  .p-lede {
    max-width: 58ch;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--color-ink-secondary);
  }

  .p-updated {
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-ink-tertiary);
  }

  .p-body {
    max-width: 42rem;
    padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4rem) clamp(4rem, 8vw, 6rem);
  }

  .p-section + .p-section {
    margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
    padding-top: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
  }

  .p-section h2 {
    margin-bottom: 1.1rem;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(1.3rem, 2.4vw, 1.6rem);
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .p-section p {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: var(--color-ink-secondary);
  }

  .p-section p + p {
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .p-section strong {
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .p-section a {
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .p-list {
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.7rem;
  }

  .p-list li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--color-ink-secondary);
  }

  .p-list li::before {
    content: '—';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
  }

  .p-list + p {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }

  .p-section--emphasis {
    padding: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2rem);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-accent);
    border-radius: 4px;
    background: var(--color-surface-secondary);
  }

  .p-section--emphasis + .p-section {
    border-top: none;
  }

  .p-processors {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }

  .p-processor {
    padding: 1.1rem 1.25rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    border-radius: 3px;
    background: var(--color-surface-secondary);
  }

  .p-processor-name {
    margin-bottom: 0.45rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .p-processor p:not(.p-processor-name) {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .p-section--emphasis {
      padding: 1.25rem;
    }

    .p-processor {
      padding: 1rem;
    }
  }
</style>
